 on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Louisiana sits firmly in the Republican column at the presidential level, with a partisan lean of roughly eleven points toward Republicans. The 2028 contest is an open race: the incumbent president is term-limited, and neither party has settled on a nominee. No statewide general-election polls or expert ratings are available yet, so the forecast rests on the state's underlying partisan fundamentals.\n\nRepublican advantages here have been substantial in recent cycles, and 2028 speculation from figures like Senator John Kennedy underlines the party's deep bench. Democrats have shown some organizational energy, including redistricting fights and voting-system debates, but nothing suggests the state is competitive at the presidential level. The expected outcome is a double-digit Republican win, with meaningful uncertainty because the nominees and the national climate remain unknown.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.108, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.162, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1608, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1621, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1551, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.153, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.155, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1556, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1525, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.1509, "margin": -11.0}]}